{
  "dom_id": "step-q343349",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14096050/assets/6e41308fb734cdb42fe0d199a46e7db8edd25bd5d9ea5a3fabff0601a42dce51.png)\n\nA rectangular loaf of rising bread dough is squeezed between two rigid side panels that keep its width fixed at $60\\,\\text{mm}.$ The length of the loaf is decreasing by $2\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}$ and its height is increasing by $1\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}.$ How fast is its volume changing when its dimensions are $100\\,\\text{mm}$ in length and $30\\,\\text{mm}$ in height?\n\nThe volume is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{mm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L,$ $W,$ and $H$ be the length, width, and height of the bread loaf, respectively, and\n\n- let $V$ be the volume of the bread loaf.\n\nNote that $L,$ $H,$ and $V$ change with time, while $W$ is constant. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=100\\text{\u00a0mm},\\, & W=60\\text{\u00a0mm}, \\\\ H=30\\text{\u00a0mm},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\text{\u00a0mm/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=1\\text{\u00a0mm/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the three time-dependent variables, $V,$ $H$ and $L,$ is given by the formula for the volume of bread loaf:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ treating $W$ as a constant, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W[L(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t})+H(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t})] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\cdot L(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t})+W\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(60)(100)(1)+(60)(30)(-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =6000-3600 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =2400.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of bread loaf is increasing at a rate of $2400\\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$",
